//? Title: Checkpoint I/O: Names, Dtypes and Byte-Exact State
//? Subtitle: Validate the whole assignment set before changing any live tensor
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Restoring a model means more than loading a floating weight array: counters and text metadata
must retain their exact values too. We save named floating, Int64 and byte-encoded string state,
mutate it and restore into existing handles. A failed schema check demonstrates that validation
precedes every assignment.
)
\toc

> The method

An NPZ file stores named NPY arrays; the serving/training code gives those arrays meaning.
For each destination tensor, restore_tensors checks name, dtype and shape before copying any
value. Strict mode also rejects extra names. Values are staged so source/target aliases cannot
change later reads. Int64 counters must not pass through Float32, whose consecutive-integer range
ends at 2^24. Strings use concatenated byte values and cumulative offsets:

$$
o_0=0,\quad o_{i+1}=o_i+|s_i|,\qquad s_i=\mathrm{bytes}[o_i:o_{i+1}].
$$
\alttext(Each string occupies the half-open interval between two consecutive cumulative byte offsets.)

Lengths count bytes, not Unicode scalar values. Empty strings have equal adjacent offsets;
embedded NUL bytes are ordinary data. This archive is model state, not a process snapshot.
Use training_state/save_training_checkpoint for optimizer and batch-order continuation, and
save any additional application RNG/configuration state explicitly. Storage and restoration
cost are linear in total state bytes.

The concept diagram has a retained TikZ source in `diagrams/checkpoint_io_concept.tikz`.
Regeneration requires LaTeX with PGF/TikZ and standalone, and dvisvgm.

```{bash, results="output", file="figures/checkpoint_io_concept.svg"}
set -eu
mkdir -p build
latex -interaction=batchmode -halt-on-error -output-directory=build diagrams/checkpoint_io_concept.tikz > build/checkpoint_io_concept.compile.log 2>&1
dvisvgm --no-fonts --bbox=10 --output=build/checkpoint_io_concept.svg build/checkpoint_io_concept.dvi > build/checkpoint_io_concept.render.log 2>&1
printf 'Rendered build/checkpoint_io_concept.svg from local TikZ source.\n'
```
// result_begin:
// Rendered build/checkpoint_io_concept.svg from local TikZ source.
// \image(figures/checkpoint_io_concept.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Validate the whole assignment set before changing any live tensor)
\alttext(Named typed state; weights, step, text Then Validate all entries; name + dtype + shape Then Stage and assign; preserve tensor handles.)

> Simple examples

The exact counter is 2^40+7. The vocabulary contains café, an embedded-NUL string and an empty string.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/checkpoint_io_strings.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
 namespace n=dm::nn;
 std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
 auto w=n::tensor({1,2,3,4},{2,2}),step=n::from_vector(std::vector<int64_t>{(int64_t{1}<<40)+7},{1});
 auto text=n::encode_strings({"café",std::string("a\0b",3),""});
 n::NamedTensors state{{"weights",w},{"step",step}};state.insert(state.end(),text.begin(),text.end());
 n::save_npz("build/checkpoint_state.npz",state);
 {n::NoGradGuard guard;w.copy_(n::full({2,2},-9));step.copy_(n::from_vector(std::vector<int64_t>{-1},{1}));}
 const auto saved=n::load_npz("build/checkpoint_state.npz");n::restore_tensors(state,saved);
 const auto words=n::decode_strings({saved[2],saved[3]});
 if(step.item<int64_t>()!=(int64_t{1}<<40)+7||words[1]!=std::string("a\0b",3))return 1;
 dm::dstruct::DataFrame t;t.add_column("entry",std::vector<std::string>{"café","a-NUL-b","empty"});
 std::vector<double> lengths;for(const auto& s:words)lengths.push_back(s.size());t.add_column("byte_length",lengths);
 auto p=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();p.bars({0,1,2},lengths,"bytes",{37,99,235},.5)
 .title("Byte lengths preserve NUL and empty strings").x_label("vocabulary entry").y_label("bytes").y_limits(0,6);
 p.size(820,420).save_svg("build/checkpoint_io_strings.svg");
 std::print("Restored Int64 counter: {}.\n\n{}\n",step.item<int64_t>(),t.to_md());
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Restored Int64 counter: 1099511627783.

| entry | byte_length |
| :--- | ---: |
| café | 5 |
| a-NUL-b | 3 |
| empty | 0 |

\image(figures/checkpoint_io_strings.svg)
// result_end
\caption(UTF-8 storage length differs from character count; NUL does not terminate a stored string.)
\alttext(UTF-8 storage length differs from character count; NUL does not terminate a stored string.)

> Checking the theory

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
 namespace n=dm::nn;
 auto a=n::tensor({1,2}),b=n::tensor({3,4});const auto before=n::encode_npy(a);int rejected=0;
 try{n::restore_tensors({{"a",a},{"b",b}},{{"a",n::tensor({9,9})},{"b",n::tensor({1})}});}catch(const std::invalid_argument&){++rejected;}
 if(n::encode_npy(a)!=before)return 1;
 try{n::restore_tensors({{"a",a}},{{"a",n::tensor({7,8},{2},n::DType::Float64)}});}catch(const std::invalid_argument&){++rejected;}
 n::restore_tensors({{"a",a},{"b",b}},{{"a",b},{"b",a}});
 if(rejected!=2||a.to_vector<int>()!=std::vector<int>({3,4})||b.to_vector<int>()!=std::vector<int>({1,2}))return 1;
 std::print("Shape/dtype mismatches rejected: {}; first assignment unchanged on failure.\n\nAliased source swap succeeds after staging deep copies.\n",rejected);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Shape/dtype mismatches rejected: 2; first assignment unchanged on failure.

Aliased source swap succeeds after staging deep copies.
// result_end


> Conclusion

Names, shapes and dtypes determine whether a snapshot can restore the intended state. The large
integer and byte strings survive exactly, and validation errors leave targets untouched. Matching
arrays do not guarantee matching model code, so retain the computation and configuration as well.

> References

- [NumPy NPY format specification](https://numpy.org/doc/stable/reference/generated/numpy.lib.format.html).
